  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "2wwyBW2YwVEqhJMfoEMscUhEZHk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:46.169Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Comtrade 360 reads as pure custom programming, which is the profile the design-partner search is looking for. At 89 people there is no in-house product occupying the application layer, so nothing internal has to be displaced for an application built on Powerhouse to sit there. The research also puts the decision inside one conversation: at that headcount the person who can say yes is reachable directly rather than through a partner-alliance function.\n\nThe single unresolved axis is open-source posture, which was not researched. That matters because every other qualifying signal here is structural \u2014 size, an empty app-layer slot, decision latency \u2014 and posture is the one thing that has to be observed rather than inferred from shape. Until it is checked the profile cannot be called ideal, only ideal-except-for-one-thing, which is why the lead sits at priority 2 rather than at the front of the queue.\n\nCommercially this is the cheapest kind of next step. A single pass over public repositories and licence choices resolves the last gap on an otherwise complete profile, and if it passes, Comtrade 360 moves up without any further research being needed.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Comtrade 360 (SI \u00b7 89 (2024))\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Pure custom programming at 89 people - nothing occupies the app-layer slot and one conversation decides. OSS posture unverified: the main gap on an otherwise ideal profile.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:46.169558Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:46.169564Z"
    }
  },
